1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,222
Clare:

2
00:00:00,222 --> 00:00:00,685
Hi Jake.

3
00:00:00,685 --> 00:00:03,850
How are you getting on with the practical teaching?

4
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:03,900
Jake:

5
00:00:03,900 --> 00:00:05,847
It’s harder than I expected,

6
00:00:05,847 --> 00:00:07,844
but I’ve got some great classes.

7
00:00:07,844 --> 00:00:08,700
How about you?

8
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:09,096
Clare:

9
00:00:09,096 --> 00:00:09,888
Not brilliant.

10
00:00:09,888 --> 00:00:13,850
I’m really struggling with my Year 12 science class.

11
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:14,147
Jake:

12
00:00:14,147 --> 00:00:15,630
Are they hard to control?

13
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:15,923
Clare:

14
00:00:15,923 --> 00:00:18,561
Well, I don’t have discipline problems as such.

15
00:00:18,561 --> 00:00:23,251
It’s just that they don’t seem to think that science has anything to do

16
00:00:23,251 --> 00:00:24,131
with their lives.

17
00:00:24,131 --> 00:00:25,010
It’s depressing.

18
00:00:25,010 --> 00:00:25,285
Clare:

19
00:00:25,285 --> 00:00:26,935
They listen to what I say,

20
00:00:26,935 --> 00:00:30,785
and I gave them a test last week and the results weren’t too

21
00:00:30,785 --> 00:00:31,060
bad,

22
00:00:31,060 --> 00:00:32,710
but there’s no real engagement.

23
00:00:32,710 --> 00:00:33,410
Jake:

24
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:34,110
Right.

25
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:34,416
Clare:

26
00:00:34,416 --> 00:00:38,693
And as part of my teaching practice, I have to design an experiment for

27
00:00:38,693 --> 00:00:39,610
them to do.

28
00:00:39,610 --> 00:00:39,877
Clare:

29
00:00:39,877 --> 00:00:42,542
I was wondering about something on the children’s diets .

30
00:00:42,542 --> 00:00:42,592
.

31
00:00:42,592 --> 00:00:42,642
.

32
00:00:42,642 --> 00:00:46,274
you know, asking them to record what they eat and maybe linking it to

33
00:00:46,274 --> 00:00:47,340
their state of health.

34
00:00:47,340 --> 00:00:47,792
Jake:

35
00:00:47,792 --> 00:00:48,244
Mmm.

36
00:00:48,244 --> 00:00:49,599
Let’s think.

37
00:00:49,599 --> 00:00:54,570
So your methodology would involve the children recording what they eat.

38
00:00:54,570 --> 00:00:54,931
Jake:

39
00:00:54,931 --> 00:00:55,292
OK,

40
00:00:55,292 --> 00:01:00,345
but you’d also need to have access to the children’s medical records

41
00:01:00,345 --> 00:01:04,315
and I don’t think people would be happy about that;

42
00:01:04,315 --> 00:01:06,120
confidentiality would be an issue.

43
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:06,436
Jake:

44
00:01:06,436 --> 00:01:10,223
If you could get the right data, the conclusions might be significant,

45
00:01:10,223 --> 00:01:13,380
but I suspect it’s not going to be easy.

46
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:13,655
Clare:

47
00:01:13,655 --> 00:01:13,930
Right.

48
00:01:13,930 --> 00:01:14,303
Jake:

49
00:01:14,303 --> 00:01:17,660
Have you thought about doing an experiment using animals?

50
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:17,947
Clare:

51
00:01:17,947 --> 00:01:20,240
Wouldn’t that be upsetting for the children?

52
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:20,553
Jake:

53
00:01:20,553 --> 00:01:24,304
Well, the animals don’t have to be harmed in any way.

54
00:01:24,304 --> 00:01:28,367
It could just be an experiment where they’re given a certain diet

55
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:29,930
and the effects are observed.

56
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:30,206
Clare:

57
00:01:30,206 --> 00:01:32,690
Would I have to get permission to use animals?

58
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:33,051
Jake:

59
00:01:33,051 --> 00:01:37,026
Yes, you’d have to submit an outline of the experiment

60
00:01:37,026 --> 00:01:38,833
and fill in a form,

61
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:40,640
but it’s quite straightforward.

62
00:01:40,640 --> 00:01:40,941
Clare:

63
00:01:40,941 --> 00:01:45,156
But if we found out that, say, a particular diet affects the health of

64
00:01:45,156 --> 00:01:49,070
animals, the same thing wouldn’t necessarily be true for people, would it?

65
00:01:49,070 --> 00:01:49,402
Jake:

66
00:01:49,402 --> 00:01:50,731
No that’s true,

67
00:01:50,731 --> 00:01:54,384
but the findings for any experiment are going to be limited.

68
00:01:54,384 --> 00:01:55,380
It’s inevitable.

69
00:01:55,380 --> 00:01:55,820
Clare:

70
00:01:55,820 --> 00:01:57,140
I suppose so.

71
00:01:57,140 --> 00:02:02,420
So what animals could I use to investigate the effects of diet?

72
00:02:02,420 --> 00:02:02,860
Mice?

73
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:03,285
Jake:

74
00:02:03,285 --> 00:02:03,711
Yes.

75
00:02:03,711 --> 00:02:09,240
You’d need experimental mice – ones that have been specially bred for experiments.

76
00:02:09,240 --> 00:02:09,717
Jake:

77
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:10,194
OK,

78
00:02:10,194 --> 00:02:14,010
so what will your experiment be investigating exactly?

79
00:02:14,010 --> 00:02:14,390
Clare:

80
00:02:14,390 --> 00:02:16,673
Well, something to do with nutrition.

81
00:02:16,673 --> 00:02:19,716
So maybe we could look at food supplements .

82
00:02:19,716 --> 00:02:19,766
.

83
00:02:19,766 --> 00:02:19,816
.

84
00:02:19,816 --> 00:02:22,378
things like extra iron and extra protein,

85
00:02:22,378 --> 00:02:24,280
and their impact on health.

86
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:24,665
Jake:

87
00:02:24,665 --> 00:02:25,051
Mmm.

88
00:02:25,051 --> 00:02:26,978
That might be rather broad.

89
00:02:26,978 --> 00:02:32,374
Maybe just look at the effects of one supplement, like sugar, on the health

90
00:02:32,374 --> 00:02:33,530
of the mice?

91
00:02:33,530 --> 00:02:33,869
Clare:

92
00:02:33,869 --> 00:02:38,621
In fact, maybe the focus could be on whether mice can control their own

93
00:02:38,621 --> 00:02:38,960
diet.

94
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:39,010
.

95
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:39,060
.

96
00:02:39,060 --> 00:02:39,110
.

97
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:39,241
Jake:

98
00:02:39,241 --> 00:02:42,046
So, what happens when they have access to more sugar,

99
00:02:42,046 --> 00:02:43,730
that they don’t really need?

100
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:44,050
Clare:

101
00:02:44,050 --> 00:02:44,370
Exactly.

102
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:47,890
Do they eat it or do they decide to leave it?

103
00:02:47,890 --> 00:02:48,337
Jake:

104
00:02:48,337 --> 00:02:48,784
Great.

105
00:02:48,784 --> 00:02:54,150
Then later on, you could do a follow-up experiment adding another variable.

106
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:54,460
Jake:

107
00:02:54,460 --> 00:03:00,040
Like, you could give some of the mice the chance to be more active, running on a wheel

108
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:00,660
or something,

109
00:03:00,660 --> 00:03:04,070
and the others just sit around and don’t do much.

110
00:03:04,070 --> 00:03:04,448
Clare:

111
00:03:04,448 --> 00:03:09,737
Or I could repeat the experiment but change the type of food I provided .

112
00:03:09,737 --> 00:03:09,787
.

113
00:03:09,787 --> 00:03:09,837
.

114
00:03:09,837 --> 00:03:12,760
or use mice with a different genetic structure.

115
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,159
Clare:

116
00:03:13,159 --> 00:03:18,751
But I think your idea would be more interesting, I might think about that

117
00:03:18,751 --> 00:03:19,550
some more.

118
00:03:19,550 --> 00:03:19,818
Clare:

119
00:03:19,818 --> 00:03:23,568
So can I talk through a possible procedure for the experiment where mice are

120
00:03:23,568 --> 00:03:24,640
given a sugar supplement?

121
00:03:24,640 --> 00:03:24,987
Jake:

122
00:03:24,987 --> 00:03:25,334
Sure.

123
00:03:25,334 --> 00:03:28,110
I did a similar experiment in college actually.

124
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:28,503
Clare:

125
00:03:28,503 --> 00:03:28,897
Great.

126
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:31,650
So how many mice would I need?

127
00:03:31,650 --> 00:03:32,002
Jake:

128
00:03:32,002 --> 00:03:33,761
I’d say about 12.

129
00:03:33,761 --> 00:03:37,630
And all young ones, not a mixture of old and young.

130
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:38,033
Clare:

131
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:38,437
OK.

132
00:03:38,437 --> 00:03:42,068
And I’d need two groups of equal sizes,

133
00:03:42,068 --> 00:03:44,086
so six in each group.

134
00:03:44,086 --> 00:03:46,910
And how would I tell them apart?

135
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:47,228
Clare:

136
00:03:47,228 --> 00:03:51,050
I suppose I could put some sort of tag on one group .

137
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:51,100
.

138
00:03:51,100 --> 00:03:51,151
.

139
00:03:51,151 --> 00:03:53,280
or just mark them in some way?

140
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:53,542
Jake:

141
00:03:53,542 --> 00:03:54,851
You could use food colouring,

142
00:03:54,851 --> 00:03:56,160
that wouldn’t hurt them.

143
00:03:56,160 --> 00:03:56,534
Clare:

144
00:03:56,534 --> 00:03:56,907
Perfect.

145
00:03:56,907 --> 00:04:00,270
Then each group would go into a separate cage,

146
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:05,500
and one group, let’s call them group A, would be the control group.

147
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:05,782
Clare:

148
00:04:05,782 --> 00:04:08,038
So they’d just have ordinary mouse food.

149
00:04:08,038 --> 00:04:09,730
I suppose you can buy that?

150
00:04:09,730 --> 00:04:10,070
Jake:

151
00:04:10,070 --> 00:04:12,110
Yes, it comes in dry pellets.

152
00:04:12,110 --> 00:04:12,368
Clare:

153
00:04:12,368 --> 00:04:15,459
And the other group would have the same as the first group,

154
00:04:15,459 --> 00:04:17,520
but they’d also have the extra sugar.

155
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:17,812
Jake:

156
00:04:17,812 --> 00:04:19,860
Would you just give them straight sugar?

157
00:04:19,860 --> 00:04:20,112
Clare:

158
00:04:20,112 --> 00:04:23,140
It might be better to give them something like cereal with it.

159
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:23,482
Jake:

160
00:04:23,482 --> 00:04:23,823
Mmm.

161
00:04:23,823 --> 00:04:28,608
Then you’d need to weigh the mice, I should think once a week.

162
00:04:28,608 --> 00:04:31,000
And you’d need an electronic balance.

163
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:31,251
Clare:

164
00:04:31,251 --> 00:04:33,506
But we can’t hold them on the balance,

165
00:04:33,506 --> 00:04:35,010
or it’d affect the reading.

166
00:04:35,010 --> 00:04:35,394
Jake:

167
00:04:35,394 --> 00:04:35,777
Exactly.

168
00:04:35,777 --> 00:04:41,146
So you need something called a weighing chamber to stop the mice from running

169
00:04:41,146 --> 00:04:41,530
away.

170
00:04:41,530 --> 00:04:41,872
Jake:

171
00:04:41,872 --> 00:04:42,899
It sounds complicated,

172
00:04:42,899 --> 00:04:47,690
but actually you can just use a plastic box with holes in the top.

173
00:04:47,690 --> 00:04:48,033
Clare:

174
00:04:48,033 --> 00:04:48,377
OK.

175
00:04:48,377 --> 00:04:53,529
So once we’ve measured the weight gain of each mouse we can work out

176
00:04:53,529 --> 00:04:57,307
the average for each group, as well as the standard deviation.

177
00:04:57,307 --> 00:05:00,055
And then see where we go from there.

178
00:05:00,055 --> 00:05:03,490
That sounds cool, I think the students will enjoy it.

179
00:05:03,490 --> 00:05:03,861
Jake:

180
00:05:03,861 --> 00:05:04,233
Yes.

181
00:05:04,233 --> 00:05:06,090
One thing you must remember .

182
00:05:06,090 --> 00:05:06,140
.

183
00:05:06,140 --> 00:05:06,190
.
